t  talking if the if the if the baseline is  to say everything we are that we  have agreed before  is to be put aside so so we're at  stalemate really aren't we  for the moment we are for the moment we  are but let me say that we are very  committed to finding solutions we've  been to northern ireland several times  we talk all the time to northern ireland  stakeholders as much as we talk to our  interlocutors here in london and i think  we found  solutions for the problems or the main  problems that we found in implementing  this protocol we are about to reduce our  label and willing to reduce by 80  the sanitary checks by 50 the paperwork  on people you know normally if we apply  the rules by the book a lordy that  carries different kinds of goods will  have hundreds of pages of paperwork we  have reduced it to less than three pages  per lorry  but  there has to be controls because this is  about  as much as protecting the good friday  agreement to which we are very committed  but it's also about protecting the  single market of the european union all  right well we'll watch this space um  let's turn to  one of the biggest stories still and  that is ukraine it's um announced this  week that ukraine has been granted eu  candidate status  chancellor scholl so germany says the  eu's got to reform its voting rules  before it can admit new member countries  um  it feels that though it's a great  gesture the likelihood of ukraine  joining the eu anytime soon  seems rather small well uh trevor let's  pause uh one second about the historic  dimension of what we did this week in in  in brussels what our leaders agree it's  not not only giving ukraine moldova and  georgia a clear european perspective  meaning you are part of the family you  can be members of the european union it  was given  ukraine and moldova  candidate status meaning that from  this week they are candidates to join  the european union there's a long path  ahead of course if i take my own case of  portugal small country in the west in  the west of europe it took us nine years  uh to get a full membership of the  european union it implies a lot of  reforms it implies a lot of alignment of  of policies and structures and  legislation and rules inside ukraine but  the signal we're given the signal we're  giving this week is absolutely crucial  if you went asked me a year ago if  that that will be a likelihood before  the invasion before everything that  happened i would be very skeptical about  it today i'm i'm enthusiastic about the  prospect of giving our ukrainian friends  who are suffering so much this clear  european perspective well you say  there's a clear european perspective but  i think that you will understand it  ukrainians are taking this with a little  bit pinch of salt when um  the member states themselves haven't  presented an exactly united front mr  macron wants to give vladimir putin uh  concessions and it's on the phone to him  all the time as far as we can make out  germany  accused of dragging its feet over  weapons supplies  you can see why president zielinski  desperately wants to join the eu but  feels maybe the eu  is supportive  in words but not necessarily in deeds we  are supportive but we are also  responsible the last thing i think we  should  provide the ukrainians with is is false  hopes or false promises we need to be  realistic accession to full membership  will take time it will take effort on  the ukrainian side it will also  force us to certainly adapt some of our  structures some of our policies in order  to you know it's a 44 million people  country large territory  coming into the union of course this  requires efforts on both sides but let  me be clear this was a unanimous  decision and i think it's very important  to note our countries have evolved you  you name some some of our leaders that  maybe a few months ago had more doubts  than they had this week so the union  went a long way to address uh the the  needs and the requests and the  aspirations of the ukrainian uh  ukrainian people i i i and you're a  diplomat and i know you uh would shy  away from anything that he seemed even  mildly critical of any of the eu's  members but um  over the past week gazprom the russian  gas supplier  it's it's cut the supply through the  nordstrom pipeline by 60  um  do you think  that the russians could actually  undermine eu solidarity because frankly  the germans depend on that bulgarians  depend on on that  and that  this is pressure that could crack that  solidarity open travel let me  look back to the last few months  if if president putin's hope and goal  was to divide us if his goal and hope  was to divide nato if it was to divide  the uk from the eu on this type of  issues it totally failed we have  likely enlarged nato we have a  strengthened european union we have very  good cooperation with uk as far as  ukraine and russia is concerned and we  have adopted six packages of sanctions  you know targeting more than 1 200  people 100 entities we are cutting off  the the supply of oil  by the end of the year and of course on  gas you  you mentioned get this is extremely  sensitive for our country so there's one  thing we should never do  is to apply sanctions that hurt us more  then it hurt  our main target and we need to make sure  that we we we sustain the support of our  voters  to to the sanctions so they are  calibrated they are measured but they  are i can guarantee you hitting very  hard the heart of the russian economy  all right let's turn to a story which um  we've just been talking about with uh mr  lewis and also with mr lammy and that is  a report that the  government is planning to introduce  steel tariffs to protect the uk steel  industry um some people believe that  these would break the wto world trade  organization rules um  we are staring down the barrel of a  trade dispute possibly a trade war  aren't we  well well i don't know the measures i  cannot comment on them but in general  terms uh i don't think protectionism is  the solution for any of our problems of  course we need to be attentive to the  rules of the game if everybody in in the  in the world trade scene is following  them or not and when we find this kind  of when we find this kind of  non-compliance we are very tough as well  when it needs be so again i cannot  comment on this but i think our  economies are facing a very difficult  very difficult times largely because you  we are just coming out of the kovitz  cycle and trying to implement very  ambitious recovery uh measures and we  are hit by the consequences of the war  which by the way are not only hitting us  if you look at the seed food so food  security situation in the world i mean  the russian troops are occupying the  fields they are blocking the ports they  are destroying the infrastructures in  ukraine and they are assuming a very  heavy load of responsibility in terms of  creating possibly a wave of famine in  the world so it all goes back to  to what's happening in ukraine and  russia that's why it's so important that  we remain united the eu and uk and we're  doing a good job on that the eu itself  nato the whole western world but also  that we bring on board countries from  the south  because this is also their their concern  you um  are coming to the end of your term here  you'll be leaving moving on uh  moving on to something even more  glorious at the end of this year that's  exactly the case more glorious  you said that you believe in life after  brexit um  that's true for britain but it's also  true for the european union yeah what  what do you envisage as the future for  this large european union with all the  strains not just the the war the ukraine  war but all the other things that are  going on  how is the eu going to hold together i  think i'm i'm i'm  maybe more optimistic than i was maybe a  couple of years ago what i've seen in  our reaction to brexit where we stayed  united no other country 